// BlockKeyedTable — THE compiler's dense table keyed by `BlockId`, with a parallel presence column.
// One implementation, TWO instantiations and three users: the encoder's chunk-local block offsets
// (`BlockOffsetTable`, Targets/Shared, read by both native backends), and the inverse of a
// function's `blockRefs` (`BlockIndexById`, built by `IrModule.blockIndexById`), which the wasm
// dispatcher and the front end's await-linearity walk both read.
//
// ⭐ WHY A DENSE ARRAY AND NOT A `Map` — this is the fix for a MEASURED QUADRATIC, not a preference,
// and it is the CLASS's rationale rather than any one instantiation's, which is why it is stated
// here. `IrModule.addBlock` numbers blocks PER FUNCTION from `func.nextBlockId`, so a function's
// block ids are exactly `0 .. nextBlockId-1`: a dense index space, which is the one shape a hash
// adds nothing to. What it added instead was a quadratic, by a mechanism any int-keyed `Map` in
// this compiler could fall into:
//
//   `int.hash()` is the IDENTITY (`self and 0xFFFFFFFF`) and `Map` is open-addressed with LINEAR
//   PROBING, so a key's home slot was `id and (capacity - 1)`. A function's ids are two contiguous
//   runs — the original blocks, then the blocks the register allocator's critical-edge split
//   appended — and TARGET-tier `blockRefs` LAYOUT order interleaves them (`0 1 2049 2 3 2050 …`).
//   Until the table's capacity EXCEEDED the id range, the high run WRAPPED onto the low run's slots
//   and the two collapsed into one solid cluster that every insert had to walk to the end of.
//
//   Replaying a real 3,073-block function's id sequence through the `Map` algorithm, probes per
//   insert broken down by the capacity in force at the time:
//
//        cap    16    32    64   128   256   512  1024  2048 | 4096  8192
//        /ins  3.33  8.67 16.67 32.67 64.67 128.67 256.67 512.67 | 1.00  1.00
//
//   It was `capacity/4 + 2/3` exactly, and it fell to ONE the instant capacity (4096) first exceeded
//   the id range (3,073). Capacity is proportional to the block count, so probes-per-insert was too,
//   and the fill was O(blocks²): **171.44 probes per insert** averaged over the function, against
//   1.00 for the same keys inserted in sorted order and 6.11 for the same total blocks split across
//   64 small functions. It allocated nothing, so the memory-only instrument read it as flat for the
//   whole history of the phase; the CPU column is what saw it.
//
//   The LOOKUPS were never the problem, and that asymmetry is the tell: by the time the fixups were
//   resolved the table had reached its FINAL capacity, past the id range, so every key sat at its
//   home slot. `resolveChunkRel32Fixups` measured FLAT across the same ladder that showed the
//   inserts growing 4.3x.
//
// ⚠ **`stdlib`'s `spreadHash` KILLED THAT QUADRATIC AND DID NOT MAKE THE `Map` THE RIGHT STRUCTURE
// HERE — IT MADE IT MEASURABLY WORSE.** Since `4def53ced`, `Map`/`Set` avalanche a hash before it
// picks a slot, so no key ORDER can cluster. But a dense ascending id run is the case an identity
// hash gets PERFECT — every key at its home slot, zero collisions — and spreading it trades that for
// the ordinary open-addressing average at a 3/4 load factor. Replaying the real key sequences of a
// 2,808-function corpus through the `Map` algorithm both ways (optimizer, 2026-07-26):
//
//        Std-tier blockRefs → block.id, 2,808 functions, largest 19,201 blocks
//          identity hash            1.000 probes/insert
//          spreadHash (today)       3.088 probes/insert
//        Maxon-tier, 24 await-bearing functions of 1,201 blocks each
//          identity hash            1.000 probes/insert
//          spreadHash (today)       3.062 probes/insert
//
//   So on this key shape the class-level fix COSTS ~3x the probes it protects against, on top of the
//   hash itself, the 3/4-load rehash chain (~log2(N) full table rebuilds) and a key column, a value
//   column, a state column and a hash column where an array needs two. **The per-site "tidy O(n)"
//   was always the better answer here and it still is** — the index space is dense, the key IS the
//   index, and both operations become one bounds-checked array access with no hash at all.
//
// ⭐ WHY IT IS ONE GENERIC TYPE AND NOT A COPY PER VALUE TYPE. The array-pair-with-presence-column
// shape was written out by hand once (for `CodeOffset`), and converting the two surviving
// `Map with (BlockId, …)` sites would have written it a third and fourth time — three near-identical
// bodies a reviewer can only read as drift. The value type is the ONLY thing that differs between
// them, which is exactly what a type parameter is for. It lives under `Compiler/IR/` and not under
// `Compiler/Targets/Shared/` beside its first user because `BlockId` is an IR concept and
// `SemanticCheck` is FRONT-END: the front end must not reach into `Targets/`, and `Targets/` may
// freely reach into `IR/`, so IR is the only layer all three users can share.
//
// ⭐ WHY `present` IS A PARALLEL BOOL COLUMN AND NOT A SENTINEL `Value`. For every instantiation
// there is a legitimate value that a sentinel would have to steal: 0 is a REAL chunk offset (the
// first block of every chunk is at 0) and a REAL `blockRefs` position (the entry block). So no value
// can mean "absent", and the check the `Map` gave for free must be kept explicitly: a fixup naming a
// block this chunk never laid down, or a branch to a block that is not in this function, is a
// lowering bug and has to stay LOUD rather than silently resolving to entry.
//
// ⭐ WHY NOTHING SUPPLIES A FILL VALUE. `Array.growFilled` makes the caller supply the element the
// type cannot invent, and a generic has none to offer — so a fill parameter would have propagated an
// `unset: 0` out to all sixteen `place` sites to mean nothing. Instead the gap an out-of-order
// `place` opens is filled with the value BEING PLACED: an arbitrary, unreadable entry, because
// `present` is the only thing that says a slot means anything. `forBlockCount` likewise only sizes
// `present` (`false` is a real "absent", so `bool` needs no help) and `reserve`s the value column,
// which keeps the table at ONE allocation per column.
// ⚠ That makes a MANAGED `Value` retain one reference per gap slot until the table dies. Both
// instantiations are scalars; a managed one would need a different filling rule.
export type BlockKeyedTable uses Value
	typealias BlockValueColumn = Array with Value

	var values as BlockValueColumn
	var present as BoolArray

	// An empty table that grows to fit whatever ids are placed in it. For the hand-assembled runtime
	// chunks, which mint their labels one at a time (`X64RuntimeAsm.newLabel`) and have no count to
	// size from. `place` extends geometrically, so growing this way is amortized linear.
	export static function create() returns Self
		return Self{values: BlockValueColumn.create(), present: BoolArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// A table sized for a function whose block ids run `0 .. blockCount-1` — one allocation per
	// column, and `place` never reallocates. `IrFunction.nextBlockId` IS that count.
	export static function forBlockCount(blockCount BlockId) returns Self
		var table = Self.create()
		table.values.reserve(blockCount as ElementIndex)
		table.present.growFilled(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: false)
		return table
	end 'forBlockCount'

	// Record `value` for block `id`.
	//
	// ⚠ Re-placing an id OVERWRITES rather than refusing, and NO CALLER DEPENDS ON THAT — every one of
	// the seventeen `place` sites writes each id exactly once. The encoders walk `func.blockRefs`,
	// whose ids are unique because `IrModule.addBlock` mints them from a per-function counter; the
	// hand-assembled runtime chunks mint each label from `X64RuntimeAsm.newLabel` into a table created
	// fresh per chunk, so a label CONSTANT like `ShimDoneLabel` is reused ACROSS chunks and never twice
	// within one. What the overwrite therefore absorbs is a DUPLICATE BlockId — which re-points every
	// branch to the first block at the second, exactly the failure
	// `TargetLiveness.buildFuncTopology` asserts against at length. That assertion runs only on the
	// NATIVE path: wasm and the front end reach this table without it. Refusing a re-place here would
	// close that gap for all three users, and is a contract change rather than a comment.
	export function place(id BlockId, value Value)
		let needed = id + 1
		self.present.growFilled(needed as ElementIndex, value: false)

		// ⭐ **`value` REACHES EXACTLY ONE STORE, AND THAT IS AN OWNERSHIP RULE RATHER THAN A TIDY-UP.** An
		// extension fills `[count, needed)` and `id` IS `needed - 1`, so when the column grows `growFilled`
		// has ALREADY written slot `id` — the `set` that used to follow it unconditionally wrote the same
		// value into the same slot a second time. For a concrete element that is merely redundant; for the
		// type parameter `Value` it is refused (E3102), because the shared generic body compiles once for
		// every instantiation and a store of an opaque `T` may INHERIT this frame's one reference rather
		// than take its own, which two stores would then release twice. So `set` is the arm for an id
		// ALREADY inside the column — exactly the case `growFilled` declines to write, since it no-ops when
		// `newLength <= existing`.
		if needed > self.values.count() 'extendsColumn'
			self.values.growFilled(needed as ElementIndex, value: value)
		end 'extendsColumn' else 'alreadySized'
			try self.values.set(id, value: value) otherwise panic("BlockKeyedTable.place: values.set OOB for block {id} — the column already holds at least {needed} slots")
		end 'alreadySized'

		try self.present.set(id, value: true) otherwise panic("BlockKeyedTable.place: present.set OOB for block {id} — the column was just extended to {needed}")
	end 'place'

	// The value recorded for block `id`, or `absent` when nothing was ever placed for it. Every
	// caller turns that into a panic naming its own fixup or branch, which is more use than one
	// message here could be.
	export function get(id BlockId) returns Value throws BlockTableLookupError
		// An id PAST the column and an id inside it that nothing placed are the same answer, so they
		// share the one `throw`: `forBlockCount` sizes `present` to the function's id space while the
		// value column fills as ids arrive, which makes "past the end" an ordinary way to be absent
		// rather than a second condition.
		let known = try self.present.get(id) otherwise false

		if not known 'notPlaced'
			throw BlockTableLookupError.absent
		end 'notPlaced'

		return try self.values.get(id) otherwise panic("BlockKeyedTable.get: present says block {id} was placed but the value column has no slot for it — `place` extends both columns together, so they can only disagree if one was extended without the other")
	end 'get'
end 'BlockKeyedTable'

// Why a `get` can fail: nothing was ever placed for that block id.
export enum BlockTableLookupError implements Error
	absent
end 'BlockTableLookupError'
